{"step_index":472,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-24T18:16:35Z","content":"Created At: 2026-10-24T23:46:35+05:30\nCompleted At: 2026-10-24T23:46:35+05:30\nShowing lines 1-157 of 157\n1: # HackerRank Orchestrate - October 2026\n2: \n3: ## Ghost Data Still Exists\n4: \n5: Build an AI agent that audits whether a user's personal data was actually deleted.\n6: \n7: A deletion workflow may report **Complete** while data still exists in databases, logs, support attachments, exports, search indexes, archives, or backups. Sync and restore processes may also bring deleted data back.\n8: \n9: For each request, the agent must first determine whether deletion was actually requested. It then returns `COMPLETE`, `INCOMPLETE`, or `NOT_APPLICABLE`, reports any remaining data or recreation risk, and produces the ordered remediation and verification steps required to finish an authorized deletion safely.\n10: \n11: Read [`problem_statement.md`](./problem_statement.md) for the complete challenge contract.\n12: \n13: ---\n14: \n15: ## Quick Start\n16: \n17: Clone the repository and move into the project directory:\n18: \n19: ```bash\n20: git clone https://github.com/interviewstreet/hackerrank-orchestrate-october26.git\n21: cd hackerrank-orchestrate-october26\n22: ```\n23: \n24: Start your AI coding tool from the **repository root** so it automatically reads [`AGENTS.md`](./AGENTS.md) and logs your session turns to `log.txt` in the root directory.\n25: \n26: Build your solution in `code/main.py`, or use another language and document its setup and run command clearly in your README.\n27: \n28: Your solution must:\n29: \n30: - Read the input files from `dataset/`\n31: - Generate one prediction row for every request in `dataset/deletion_requests.csv`\n32: - Write the final predictions to `output.csv` in the repository root\n33: \n34: Run the starter Python entry point with:\n35: \n36: ```bash\n37: python3 code/main.py\n38: ```\n39: \n40: The blank template at `dataset/output.csv` is provided as a reference. Your final generated file for submission must be the root-level `output.csv`.\n41: \n42: \n43: ## Dataset\n44: \n45: All participant-facing files are in `dataset/`:\n46: \n47: | File | Contents |\n48: | --- | --- |\n49: | `deletion_requests.csv` | 518 requests |\n50: | `sample_deletion_requests.csv` | 32 sample requests |\n51: | `sample_output.csv` | Correct sample answers |\n52: | `user_identities.csv` | Current user identities |\n53: | `user_audit_log.csv` | Historical identity changes |\n54: | `system_activity.csv` | Activity across services and data processes |\n55: | `deletion_rules.csv` | Required treatment for each data category |\n56: | `evidence_files.csv` | Manifest of supplied evidence files |\n57: | `output.csv` | Blank output template |\n58: \n59: Evidence is stored under `dataset/evidence/` and may include PDFs, images, logs, JSON, plain text, or SQL snapshot exports. SQL captures may contain a primary database, regional replicas, a reporting replica, or a failover replica so replication drift can be investigated directly. The `.sql` files are readable as text and executable with SQLite.\n60: \n61: Only `deletion_requests.csv` requires answers. The other files provide evidence and context.\n62: \n63: ---\n64: \n65: ## Output\n66: \n67: Create `output.csv` with these columns in this exact order:\n68: \n69: ```text\n70: case_id,deletion_status,steps_json\n71: ```\n72: \n73: Produce exactly one row per request. `deletion_status` is `COMPLETE`, `INCOMPLETE`, or `NOT_APPLICABLE`. `steps_json` is an ordered JSON array containing findings, remediation actions, and verification checks. Use an empty array (`[]`) for `NOT_APPLICABLE`. Evidence references may be a `file_id`, `activity_id`, or `audit_id`. Determine the resource state at `audit_at` from the available evidence; reported workflow statuses and operation results are claims. Use `dataset/sample_output.csv` as the format reference.\n74: \n75: The final file must contain 518 data rows sorted by `case_id` (plus the header row). The grader parses `steps_json` as JSON, so object-key order and whitespace do not matter; array order does. Order findings, remediation, and verification in that phase order. Within a phase, follow the operation order in the problem statement, then sort by `system_id` and `resource_id`.\n76: \n77: ---\n78: \n79: ## Evaluation\n80: \n81: `output.csv` will be compared with hidden canonical answers. Scoring considers:\n82: \n83: - deletion assessment accuracy;\n84: - current and historical identity resolution;\n85: - discovery of remaining data and recreation risks;\n86: - correct policy application and evidence references; and\n87: - completeness and ordering of remediation and verification steps.\n88: \n89: Your `code.zip` must include:\n90: \n91: ```text\n92: code/evaluation/evaluation_report.md\n93: code/evaluation/usage_report.md\n94: ```\n95: \n96: - `evaluation_report.md` must cover case, row, field, and sequence accuracy, at least three additional evaluation cases created by you, failures found, and improvements made.\n97: - `usage_report.md` must cover the final full-dataset run, including model providers and names, model calls, input and output tokens, total and average tokens per request, and estimated total and per-request cost. Include per-model and overall totals when using multiple models.\n98: \n99: **Important:** Place `evaluation_report.md` and `usage_report.md` inside `code/evaluation/` (matching `code/evaluation/main.py` in this starter layout), write your complete evaluation metrics, custom evaluation test cases, and token/cost breakdowns directly inside those Markdown files, and keep prompts in source code or Markdown files. Automated code evaluation ignores raw data files (`.csv`, `.tsv`, `.json`, `.jsonl`, `.sql`, `.log`), so content stored only in those formats will not be visible to the code judge or AI interviewer.\n100: \n101: ---\n102: \n103: ## Chat Transcript